## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical thinking for real-time services, focusing on WebSocket handshakes, pub/sub latency, and backpressure—common beginner pitfalls when scaling.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket handshake overhead
- Publish/subscribe latency
- Backpressure and queue depth
- Simple workload ramping
- Component isolation for bottleneck diagnosis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton k6 WebSocket test
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const res = ws.connect('wss://chat.example.test/ws', {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () {
      socket.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'msg', text: 'hello' }));
    });
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you determine if latency is in handshake vs Redis vs DB?
- What would your SLO look like for 99th percentile latency under peak load?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of serverless vs. containerized performance under real-world burst scenarios, including cold-start effects, multi-region observability, and cost trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Cold-start impact on tail latency
- End-to-end latency measurement (P99)
- Autoscaling behavior across regions
- Cost implications of burst traffic
- Data strategy for realistic workloads

## Code Example

```javascript
async function measureLatency(url, trials = 100) {
  const start = Date.now();
  for (let i = 0; i < trials; i++) {
    await fetch(url, { cache: 'no-store' });
  }
  const end = Date.now();
  return (end - start) / trials;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you isolate cold-start latency from other backend delays in your telemetry?
- What specific metrics and dashboards would you use to validate cross-region SLA compliance during bursts?

## Why This Is Asked

